WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:05.830
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:10.200
Try to go back in time and
imagine yourself as a baby.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.600
I'm not going to ask you to start
crying or crawling around the room.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.430
But try to think of
the major learnings you had.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:29.010
Perhaps it was learning how to walk,
or how to hold an object by yourself.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:34.345
Maybe it was learing how not
to hit your baby friends.

00:00:34.345 --> 00:00:38.709
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or even how to say your first words.

00:00:41.076 --> 00:00:47.420
But let me ask you, by a show of hands,
how many of you were taught how to see?

00:00:51.334 --> 00:00:54.951
No one, right?
You shouldn't blame your parents for that.

00:00:54.951 --> 00:00:58.586
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My point here is that human sight is

00:00:58.586 --> 00:01:01.560
usually taken for granted.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.860
Unless you lose it for some reason, or

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.590
unless you were born with an eye
impairment, which was my case.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.280
To your friendly eyes, it might seem
that I don't have any problem, but

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:19.050
I was born blind from my left eye and
I have low vision in my right one.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.950
So I'd like to welcome you to my world.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:29.990
That's probably a very good
approximation of how I see things.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.370
In this case, this is not a thing, but

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:37.250
actually the love of my
life on our wedding day.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.240
But the fact is that since I was a kid,

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.960
I had to understand how
to go through that blur.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.930
I had to learn how to see.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:57.388
Now you might be asking,
what learning how to see looks like?

00:01:57.388 --> 00:02:02.200
What is seeing?

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.160
Well, biology has an explanation for us.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.970
It says that it's all about light.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.990
Light that goes through a series of
very delicate structures in our eye,

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:17.500
like the cornea or the pupil,
until it finally reaches our retina.

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:23.980
A photo sensitive tissue that transforms
light into electrical signals.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.820
Those electrical signals
then are possessed and

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:32.470
travel from our eye to our brain.

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:39.374
So in fact, our eyes just collect visual

00:02:39.374 --> 00:02:45.067
information, but we see with our brain.

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:50.473
According to science,
vision is an activity

00:02:50.473 --> 00:02:55.467
90% mental and only 10% physical.

00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:59.697
So when I look back into my struggles,

00:02:59.697 --> 00:03:04.067
I see now that biology is indeed right.

00:03:04.067 --> 00:03:11.573
And the act of seeing is much more in
here, and maybe in here, than in here.

00:03:11.573 --> 00:03:17.183
I truly believe that real sight
is based on three main attitudes,

00:03:17.183 --> 00:03:21.574
completely unrelated to
our physical condition.

00:03:21.574 --> 00:03:25.374
And I hope to share them with you today,

00:03:25.374 --> 00:03:30.815
through some of the lessons
that I had in my life.

00:03:30.815 --> 00:03:35.621
My first lesson came pretty early,
at the age of four.

00:03:35.621 --> 00:03:40.519
Trying to help me be part in
the world in a different way,

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:44.595
my parents decided to put
me in a music school.

00:03:44.595 --> 00:03:48.150
And before even learning
how to read words,

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:53.727
by transforming those little black
blurry stains into music notes,

00:03:53.727 --> 00:03:57.683
I was able to see a brand
new world through sound.

00:03:59.381 --> 00:04:02.912
But seeing that world
required a lot of effort,

00:04:02.912 --> 00:04:06.015
a lot of practice and a lot of dedication.

00:04:06.015 --> 00:04:12.820
So I learned that seeing is actually about

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:18.480
the level of depth you put in
everything you do in your life.

00:04:21.045 --> 00:04:24.230
My second lesson came some
years later than that.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.214
I was 17 years old, I was very shy.

00:04:27.214 --> 00:04:31.440
Probably facing a lot of the same
challenges you've all faced at that age.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:36.070
But I had this crazy idea of
auditioning to a theater company.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:41.678
But crazier than me were the ones who
accepted an actor who could barely see

00:04:41.678 --> 00:04:43.700
the to the other side of the stage.

00:04:45.544 --> 00:04:51.665
But at that same stage,
with a bunch of light in my face,

00:04:51.665 --> 00:04:57.680
I learned that seeing is
much more about perspective.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:04.370
By looking to the world through the eyes
of many, many different characters,

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:11.920
I learned how different people are, and
yet, how similar we are to each other.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:17.210
My third and
final lesson came more recently.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:21.730
I've always been passionate
about photography.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:25.930
Maybe because images were always
a bit mysterious and blurred to me,

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:26.940
I couldn't see them well.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:32.300
But I never thoughy I would be able
to use that professional camera to

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:33.610
take professional photographs.

00:05:35.644 --> 00:05:40.863
Well recently I met a photographer,
and he told me, have you ever noticed

00:05:40.863 --> 00:05:45.760
that you only need one eye to look
through the view finder of a camera?

00:05:47.681 --> 00:05:48.810
Indeed, he's right.

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:56.290
So this picture you're seeing here, was
taken by a photographer with just one eye.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.030
You're looking at him right now.

00:06:01.397 --> 00:06:05.868
So through my photographs,
I also learned that

00:06:05.868 --> 00:06:10.690
vision is much more about
focusing in your dreams.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:15.736
Focusing in what matters most to you,
and why.

00:06:19.348 --> 00:06:23.763
&gt;&gt; I truly believe that all of those
lessons should be taught to us together

00:06:23.763 --> 00:06:27.380
with that biology class I just
showed you some slides ago.

00:06:30.151 --> 00:06:35.416
And I'm sure that you all have already
experienced at least one moment in your

00:06:35.416 --> 00:06:40.360
lives where you didn't use even
those 10% to actually see the world.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:45.050
Don't you remember that very first kiss

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:48.020
when you almost automatically
closed your eyes?

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:51.935
I'm sure you were very,
very focused at that moment.

00:06:51.935 --> 00:06:56.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; How about the dozens of times your

00:06:56.360 --> 00:07:01.265
friends asked you to close your eyes,
just to hit you with a surprise?

00:07:01.265 --> 00:07:05.473
From a brand new perspective.

00:07:05.473 --> 00:07:08.928
Not to mention,
watching a concert where your favorite

00:07:08.928 --> 00:07:11.950
rock star closes his eyes
at the peak of the song.

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:17.090
I'm sure he was seeing a lot
of stuff in that moment, and

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:19.480
experiencing the deepest emotions.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:28.160
Hellen Keller was an American educator and
an activist for the blind the deaf people.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:30.050
She was blind actually.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:38.040
And she once said, what I'm looking for
is not out there, it's in me.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:47.750
So I'd like to invite you to step into my
world, even if you have perfect sight.

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:55.140
So the next time you face a challenge,
or a hard decision, or

00:07:55.140 --> 00:08:02.080
even a simple pleasure in life, try not to
look at it only with your eyes wide open.

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:06.540
Close them, and open up your brain.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:14.000
So you can unlock a different kind of
vision, one that goes beyond that blur.

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:22.506
And how to be more focused, broaden our
perspectives, and look deeper inside.

00:08:22.506 --> 00:08:26.434
Thank you.

00:08:26.434 --> 00:08:27.809
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:27.809 --> 00:08:37.457
[MUSIC]

